## My answer to the prompt

Assuming you mean a Raspberry Pi running Ubuntu, this example uses Raspberry Pi BCM GPIO numbers and the `lgpio` C library. A regular Ubuntu PC does not provide GPIO pins; the code must run on a GPIO-capable board. The example runs each motor in one direction, reverses both, then stops them—without loops.

```cpp
#include <lgpio.h>   // Provides lgpio GPIO-control functions.
#include <iostream> // Provides error-message output.
#include <unistd.h> // Provides sleep().

int main() {
    int chip = lgGpiochipOpen(0); // Open GPIO chip 0; chip numbering can vary by board.
    if (chip < 0) {               // Check whether the chip opened successfully.
        std::cerr << "Could not open GPIO chip\n";
        return 1;
    }

    // Claim four BCM GPIO pins as outputs, initially LOW:
    // 17/18 control motor A; 22/23 control motor B.
    if (lgGpioClaimOutput(chip, 0, 17, 0) < 0 ||
        lgGpioClaimOutput(chip, 0, 18, 0) < 0 ||
        lgGpioClaimOutput(chip, 0, 22, 0) < 0 ||
        lgGpioClaimOutput(chip, 0, 23, 0) < 0) {
        std::cerr << "Could not configure GPIO outputs\n";
        lgGpiochipClose(chip);
        return 1;
    }

    lgGpioWrite(chip, 17, 1); // Motor A: IN1 HIGH, IN2 LOW = one direction.
    lgGpioWrite(chip, 18, 0);
    lgGpioWrite(chip, 22, 1); // Motor B: IN3 HIGH, IN4 LOW = one direction.
    lgGpioWrite(chip, 23, 0);
    sleep(2);                 // Keep this direction for two seconds.

    lgGpioWrite(chip, 17, 0); // Reverse motor A by swapping its input states.
    lgGpioWrite(chip, 18, 1);
    lgGpioWrite(chip, 22, 0); // Reverse motor B in the same way.
    lgGpioWrite(chip, 23, 1);
    sleep(2);                 // Keep the reverse direction for two seconds.

    lgGpioWrite(chip, 17, 0); // Both inputs LOW stops motor A (coast on typical modules).
    lgGpioWrite(chip, 18, 0);
    lgGpioWrite(chip, 22, 0); // Both inputs LOW stops motor B.
    lgGpioWrite(chip, 23, 0);

    lgGpiochipClose(chip);    // Release the GPIO chip before exiting.
    return 0;                 // Report successful program completion.
}
```

Connect the two motor terminals to the L298N’s OUT1/OUT2 and OUT3/OUT4. Connect the four GPIOs to IN1–IN4, and make the controller and Raspberry Pi grounds common. The ENA/ENB jumpers must be installed (or those enable pins must be driven HIGH) for this direction-only example. Power the motors from a suitable motor supply, **not from the Raspberry Pi GPIO pins**. A 5 V motor may receive substantially less than 5 V through an L298N because of the driver’s voltage drop. Install/link the `lgpio` library for your Ubuntu board before compiling; GPIO chip numbering and pin mappings depend on the hardware.

## Evaluation

Assistant A is clearly more responsive than Assistant B: it answers in English, gives a no-loop C++ example, and includes extensive inline comments. Its example also demonstrates two motors moving in opposite directions and then stopping.

However, A assumes a particular GPIO numbering scheme and uses the legacy `/sys/class/gpio` interface, which is deprecated and may not be available on current Ubuntu systems. The prompt does not identify the GPIO-capable board, so the answer should have stated its hardware assumptions more clearly. It should also emphasize common ground and a separate, suitable motor supply; a 5 V L298N setup can have a significant voltage drop. Its notes about ENA/ENB are useful, but do not fully address those power and wiring cautions.

Assistant B does not meet the prompt as well. It answers in Portuguese despite the English prompt, does not provide detailed notes for each code line, and its explanation contains an enormous irrelevant block of text. The wiringPi approach and installation instructions are also questionable for a current Ubuntu setup, and the code omits the header normally needed to declare `sleep()`. More importantly, parts of its explanation inaccurately describe the L298N outputs as a motor terminal being driven positive while the other is “free.” It also fails to explain the enable-pin and motor-power requirements.

Both answers would benefit from identifying the exact board and GPIO mapping, but A is substantially more helpful and relevant.

My final verdict is: [[A>>B]]

